Nucleus Co-op

Background streaming and co-op (rust/core/src/nucleus/, rust/windows/src/nucleus/)
launch games through Nucleus Co-op (https://github.com/SplitScreen-Me/splitscreenme-nucleus)
by SplitScreen.Me, GPL-3.0 licensed, which includes ProtoInput by Ilyaki, MIT
licensed. Rubylight does not bundle them: the host downloads Nucleus Co-op 2.4.2
from its GitHub release at first use and checks its size and SHA-256, or uses a
copy the user names in nucleus_path. Game handlers come from Nucleus Co-op's
handler hub and are the user's own content.

AMDNR (Neural Rendering)

The per-app Neural Rendering switch (rust/core/src/neural.rs, rust/host/src/neural.rs)
installs AMDNR by 3zwr1 (https://github.com/3zwr1/AMD-NR---OptiScaler), a GPL-3.0
licensed fork of OptiScaler, into a game's folder. Rubylight does not host, bundle,
mirror or modify any of its files: at the user's request the host downloads the
release's AMDNR-v*.zip from that GitHub release on the user's PC and, when that
package lacks the neural runtime, the same release's AMDNR-lmxxf-*.zip of the same
version (the lmxxf runtime by Kien, MIT, as the mod's authors distribute it). It
checks them against GitHub's digests and the release's SHA256SUMS.txt, and copies
the files unmodified, license texts included; from the runtime component it takes
only the runtime and its licenses. It never fetches the release's other assets (the
launcher, the danielblnc runtimes, the capture host, the Mochi component and other
components). The console
calls the feature Neural Rendering and does not brand it with the mod's name.

InputPlumber and SDL

The virtual Steam Deck controller (rust/core/src/steam_deck.rs) uses the Steam Deck's
HID report descriptors and the feature-report replies Steam expects as captured by
InputPlumber (https://github.com/ShadowBlip/InputPlumber, GPLv3), and the input
report layout and button bits from SDL's SDL_hidapi_steamdeck.c and
controller_structs.h (https://github.com/libsdl-org/SDL, zlib license).

Moonlight V+

Nightjar's soft keyboard for the host's text fields
(client/app/src/main/java/com/limelight/ui/HostKeyboard.java) follows Moonlight V+
(https://github.com/qiin2333/moonlight-vplus, GPL-3.0) at commit 5840bab3: it opens
only for focus that follows the user's own input, does not reopen a keyboard the user
closed for the same field, and moves the stream up over the keyboard with V+'s viewport
offset (RemoteTextContextPolicy.kt, ImeAvoidanceSession.kt) instead of resizing the
window.

